## An object nobody notices — and yet it is ingenious

You arrive in Mariánské Lázně, buy a spa cup, fill it at the [Cross Spring](/en/springs-overview) and set off along the colonnade. It feels like folklore: that slightly odd flat little mug with a handle you drink from.

In reality you are holding **an object that evolved over four centuries as a medical aid**. Every one of its peculiarities — the flat shape, the hollow spout, the marks along the side — came about as an answer to a specific practical problem. Nothing about it is accidental.

## At first it was about not burning yourself

The spa cup tradition goes back to the **16th century** and originated in the Karlovy Vary region. The Karlovy Vary springs are hot — and so the first "cups" were simply **glasses in a holder**, so that patients would not burn their fingers while drinking the scalding water.

Materials then changed according to what was available and affordable:

- **17th century** — stoneware vessels, ordinary mugs and bulbous onion-shaped pots
- **milk glass** — a cheap imitation of expensive porcelain
- **early 19th century** — the rise of porcelain

In Mariánské Lázně, however, that history has **a chapter of its own**. Porcelain was very expensive at the time, so from the early 19th century **glass cups** spread here on a mass scale — and glass remained dominant in Mariánské Lázně **until the mid-20th century**. The local town museum keeps a fine collection, including crystal cups from the **Moser** glassworks and a commemorative piece marking the 1904 meeting of Emperor **Franz Joseph I with the British King Edward VII**.

## How the shape gradually flattened

Look at cups in a museum display arranged by age and you will see a clear line of development:

**mug → egg-shaped or conical form with a handle → flat shape that loses the handle**

Why flat, specifically? Because **the spa guest walks with the cup**. They are not sitting at a table. A flat cup rests comfortably in the palm, does not get in the way while walking, slips into a coat pocket or handbag and does not chafe. The shape adapted to how the object is actually used — exactly as any industrial designer would describe it today.

## The hollow spout: the cleverest detail of the whole cup

The most characteristic feature — **the hollow handle that doubles as a spout** — appears only at the **end of the 19th century**. It is not decoration and not an accident. It solves several problems at once:

### 1. You drink while walking

The basic rule of the drinking cure is: **the patient should walk while drinking the mineral water**. The water is sipped slowly, in small mouthfuls, in motion. But drinking from an open mug while walking along a crowded colonnade is a reliable recipe for a soaked coat — your own and other people's. The spout solves this elegantly: you sip, you do not spill.

### 2. It protects the teeth

Mineral water is loaded with minerals and tends to be more acidic. When sipped through a straw, **it does not come into direct contact with tooth enamel**. This logic, which dentists today recommend for acidic drinks in general, was handled by our ancestors a hundred and thirty years ago — through the shape of the vessel.

### 3. You do not get burned

The original Karlovy Vary problem applies here too: you hold hot water by the spout and drink it in a thin stream instead of putting your lips to a scalding rim.

### 4. It forces you to drink slowly

And this is perhaps the loveliest part. The narrow spout **physically makes it impossible to down the cup in three gulps**. The drinking cure, however, rests precisely on slow, spread-out intake — the water should pass through the body gradually, not in bursts. So our ancestors did not enforce the correct pace by lecturing. **They built it into the vessel.** The cup drinks slowly for you, whether you like it or not.

## The marks along the side: dosage, not decoration

On older cups you will find **marks or measuring lines**. These are not decorative stripes — they are a **dosing gauge**.

The spa physician did not simply prescribe "drink mineral water". He prescribed **a specific spring, a specific quantity and a specific time**. A cup with marks was effectively a wearable prescription: the guest knew where to fill it and how far not to fill it. Even today, a [drinking cure](/en/magazine/drinking-cure-guide) should be discussed with a doctor — the springs differ in composition, and more does not mean better.

## When the tool also became a souvenir

Once you carry an object in your hand all day, you start wanting something beautiful on it. The decoration of the cups therefore went through its own evolution:

- at first just a **plain inscription with the name of the spa**
- then **painted scenes** of places, allegories of health, flowers and birds
- later **relief, three-dimensional motifs**, once porcelain production allowed it
- and in Mariánské Lázně additionally **cut crystal, coloured glass and engraving**

**Personalisation** was popular too — a guest could have initials or a full name engraved. The cup was thus simultaneously a health aid, a memento of the stay and, to a degree, a calling card.

## The wisdom in it

Put it all together and something remarkable emerges. The spa cup is **functional design long before anyone called it that**:

| Feature | Practical reason |
|---|---|
| Flat shape | Sits in the hand while walking, fits in a pocket |
| Hollow spout | Nothing spills, protects the teeth, no burns |
| Narrow spout bore | Enforces the slow drinking the cure requires |
| Marks along the side | Sticking to the prescribed dose |
| Handle / holder | Protection from hot water |
| Decoration and name | A memento that outlasts the stay |

It is not a souvenir you happen to drink from. **It is a medical device that happened to become a souvenir.**

## How to use it today

You can buy a cup on the colonnade and in shops all over town — from simple porcelain to cut glass. And then all that remains is to observe what people built into its shape a hundred and fifty years ago:

1. **Fill it at the prescribed spring** — the [springs overview](/en/springs-overview) will tell you which is which
2. **Drink slowly, in small sips** — the spout will help you
3. **Walk while you do it** — ideally along the [Main Colonnade](/en/colonnade), best of all during a promenade concert by [our orchestra](/en/magazine/zapadocesky-symfonicky-orchestr)
4. **Follow your doctor's advice** — with the [drinking cure](/en/mineral-springs) the dose really matters

Next time you take a sip, notice the shape in your hand. It is one of the few objects we use exactly as they were intended — and have done for more than a century.
